- Ended
- Temporarily disconnected
- Unknown/error

Do not mistake scheduled status for an actually live stream.

When a stream is confirmed starting/live:
1. Begin bounded rolling capture, not full-stream download.
2. Capture short segments/chunks.
3. Save live session identity and chunk timestamps/state.
4. Keep buffer/storage under configured safe limits.
5. Detect reconnects and stream restarts.
6. Use timeouts, bounded retries and backoff.
7. Do not poll indefinitely.
8. Prioritize live capture according to configured source priority.
9. Do not let one source failure block others.

Live highlight signals:
- Excited reactions
- Laughter
- Unexpected dialogue
- Gameplay events
- Clutch/action moments
- Interesting or useful content
- Suspense and payoff
- Strong reactions
- Meaningful scene changes

Do not create a clip from every signal blindly.
Check uniqueness, context, clip boundaries, quality and overlap.

While live priority is active:
- Give confirmed live capture and live highlight processing priority.
- Reduce/pause normal-video work if necessary according to safe resource
  policy.
- Avoid flooding the editing queue.
- Preserve live chunks until all dependent processing/render/QC tasks are
  safely complete.

When the live ends:
- Confirm ended/offline state.
- Finalize capture state.
- Reconcile incomplete chunks and gaps.
- Finalize candidate moments.
- Process pending live highlights safely.
- Then resume normal source scanning according to priority policy.

Recovery:
- Persist capture state.
- Record last valid chunk.
- Reconnect safely when stream is still live.
- Mark missing gaps honestly.
- Prevent duplicate segments/clips.
- Respect storage caps and backoff.
- Never delete chunks with active dependencies.
- Only remove temporary chunks after dependency checks and verified output
  completion, according to existing safe retention policy.

Multiple live sources:
- Respect configured priority and safe concurrency.
- Avoid device overload.
- Maintain isolated session identities and queues.

Real Android capture must not be claimed verified unless tested on Termux.

==================================================
14. SOURCE SCAN AND FAIRNESS
==================================================

Support multiple sources concurrently and fairly.

Modes, if supported by existing architecture:
- Live priority
- Source round-robin
- Continuous selected source
- New/recent video priority
- Source-to-account mapping
- Configured multi-route
- Fair share within explicitly mapped destinations

Rules:
- No random account routing.
- Fair rotation cannot override source mapping.
- Do not endlessly rescan the same old videos.
- Prefer new/recent eligible videos according to configured scan policy.
- Older videos may be fallback when appropriate and enabled.
- Skip failed/offline sources without blocking other sources.
- Use bounded retries/backoff.
- Prevent duplicate video analysis and candidate extraction.
- Do not create an upload flood just because clip generation is unlimited.

==================================================
15. SCHEDULING AND PUBLISHING
==================================================

Generation and publishing limits are separate.

CLIP GENERATION:
No arbitrary fixed clip count ceiling.

PUBLISHING:
Respect per-channel/per-account/per-platform schedule and configured limits.

Per destination:
- Schedule/timezone
- Daily publishing cap
- Minimum gap between posts
- Quiet hours
- Queue
- Source priority
- Live priority
- Platform rate limits
- Retry window
- Backlog handling

Rules:
- Clips remain queued when schedule slots are full.
- Do not delete clips because today's slots are full.
- Do not instantly publish queued clips to bypass schedules.
- A clip only goes to explicitly mapped destination(s).
- Multi-route destinations have independent upload states and schedules.
- Route/account mismatch blocks publishing.
- Disconnected OAuth remains pending/blocked safely.
- Retry must not cause duplicate uploads.
- Verify the destination channel and uploaded video ID.
- Public release must respect configured authorization/gates.
- Do not enable public autopilot or auto-approval automatically.
- No account reconnection or OAuth consent action without user permission.
- No automatic cross-account fallback.

Use configured timezone, typically Asia/Kolkata, and verify schedule math.

==================================================
16. METADATA AND PACKAGING
==================================================

Metadata must be based on the actual clip content and selected destination.

Generate:
- Clip-specific title
- Accurate description
- Relevant tags/keywords where supported
- Appropriate hashtags, without spam
- Thumbnail/cover if current system supports it

Requirements:
- Do not use generic repeated titles.
- Do not invent dialogue, events or claims.
- Avoid duplicate titles on the same destination.
- Do not use misleading clickbait.
- Respect channel niche/branding.
- Keep metadata appropriate to the clip.
- Metadata generation must not claim unsupported source ownership.
- Preserve existing working metadata features; audit before replacing.

==================================================
17. ANALYTICS AND CONTINUOUS LEARNING
==================================================

Do not analyze performance and then stop.

For every published clip/destination, collect actual available data:
- Views
- Likes
- Comments
- Shares, where available
- Watch time
- Average view duration
- Retention/percentage viewed, where available
- Audience response signals, where available

Missing data must be "unavailable", not fabricated as zero.

Wait for meaningful analytics windows. Avoid judging a clip too early.
Record clip ID, channel/destination, content category, duration, hook type,
editing profile/version, metadata version, schedule and actual metrics.

Analyze possible performance problems:
- Weak hook
- Wrong clip selection
- Missing context
- Too long/too short
- Pacing
- Caption accuracy/readability
- Audio/video quality
- Topic relevance
- Title/thumbnail packaging
- Posting time
- Audience mismatch
- Distribution limitations

Do not assume low views have one definite cause.
Distinguish actual evidence from hypotheses.
Compare similar clips and avoid overfitting small samples.

Learning loop:
1. Collect verified metrics.
2. Diagnose probable weakness/strength.
3. Record evidence and confidence.
4. Propose a bounded change.
5. Apply safe changes to future selection, hooks, pacing, metadata or schedule.
6. Version the change.
7. Compare results.
8. Keep, revert or refine based on evidence.
9. Continue learning.

Editing presets, transitions, effects, caption styles, fonts, templates,
render settings and locked editing code must never be changed by the
learning system.

Do not automatically alter already-published clips.
Do not promise viral views.
Optimize responsibly for viewer satisfaction, relevance, retention, watch
time and genuine engagement—not spam or deceptive engagement bait.

